    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The characterization that dissent arguments are \"legally flawed\" reflects the Supreme Court's formal rejection of their precedential logic *from the perspective of the majority itself*, not an objective verdict on absolute truth. Legally, the dissent's claim that right to abortion is a fundamental liberty protected by the Due Process Clause (as they cite *Roe* and *Casey* established) is considered flawed specifically because the majority found those prior cases legally inapplicable given the text and history of the Constitution. In this context, legitimacy is determined by whether the ruling's logic is traced to established constitutional principles; while the dissenters utilized sound legal principles to argue for stare decisis (adherence to precedent), the majority concluded that the precedent itself relied on a flawed reading of history that can no longer stand. Therefore, under the strict legal scrutiny of current constitutional text and historical evidence, the majority judges the dissent's justification as unsustainable, even if the dissenters remain the only other legitimate authority on the issue post-*Roe* overturning.",
      "source": "target",
